\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {REPA's early-training acceleration holds across the sampler-noise axis.} The representative REPA variant per dataset beats the baseline on both FPSD and designability at almost every $\gamma $; the lone exception is PDB FPSD at the degenerate $\gamma {=}0$. (Absolute values at step 400K; $n{=}3$ seeds at $\gamma {=}0.45$ [2 for AFDB], else $n{=}1$; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}}{42}{table.6.4}}
\newlabel{tab:proteina-sampler}{{6.4}{42}{\textbf {REPA's early-training acceleration holds across the sampler-noise axis.} The representative REPA variant per dataset beats the baseline on both FPSD and designability at almost every $\gamma $; the lone exception is PDB FPSD at the degenerate $\gamma {=}0$. (Absolute values at step 400K; $n{=}3$ seeds at $\gamma {=}0.45$ [2 for AFDB], else $n{=}1$; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}{table.6.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Better trunk representations track better generation --- strongly for designability, moderately for FPSD.} Partial Pearson correlation controlling for training step, with the raw value alongside; oriented so a positive value means better representation accompanies better generation. ($n{=}59$ checkpoints, one per variant and training step, pooled over the baseline, the REPA variants, and the random control; each contributes a single (representation, generation) point, with generation seed-averaged to one value ($3$ seeds, single-seed at the late tail) and the probe read at one seed. $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}}{43}{table.6.5}}
\newlabel{tab:proteina-genrep-corr}{{6.5}{43}{\textbf {Better trunk representations track better generation --- strongly for designability, moderately for FPSD.} Partial Pearson correlation controlling for training step, with the raw value alongside; oriented so a positive value means better representation accompanies better generation. ($n{=}59$ checkpoints, one per variant and training step, pooled over the baseline, the REPA variants, and the random control; each contributes a single (representation, generation) point, with generation seed-averaged to one value ($3$ seeds, single-seed at the late tail) and the probe read at one seed. $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}{table.6.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {At equal compute, our strongest variant achieves better representation and generation quality.} The dashed arrow marks the matched-compute comparison at step 400K. Against the baseline, the strongest PDB variant (REPA-L9-MPNN) has higher fold accuracy (CATH-A) and better generation: lower FPSD (a) and higher designability (b). (Markers are a coarse $\approx $400K-spaced subset of checkpoints from 100K, with the 400K point bordered; $x$ = student CATH-A accuracy, higher to the right; generation $n{=}3$ seeds, except the single-seed tail checkpoint on each trajectory; probe single-seed; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}}{43}{figure.6.4}}
\newlabel{fig:proteina-genrep}{{6.4}{43}{\textbf {At equal compute, our strongest variant achieves better representation and generation quality.} The dashed arrow marks the matched-compute comparison at step 400K. Against the baseline, the strongest PDB variant (REPA-L9-MPNN) has higher fold accuracy (CATH-A) and better generation: lower FPSD (a) and higher designability (b). (Markers are a coarse $\approx $400K-spaced subset of checkpoints from 100K, with the 400K point bordered; $x$ = student CATH-A accuracy, higher to the right; generation $n{=}3$ seeds, except the single-seed tail checkpoint on each trajectory; probe single-seed; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}{figure.6.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {On AFDB, REPA lifts the whole-set distribution cleanly; on PDB, the whole-set gains come with a more concentrated designable subset.} We compare rows within a data regime block, not across them. FPSD uses the training dataset as its reference in each case. Each cell is a mean over generation seeds; the per-seed min--max range is in Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:13m-ranges} (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:proteina-13m-ranges}). (Rows tagged with a step, e.g.\ \textit  {\relax \fontsize  {8}{9.5}\selectfont  (1.0M)}, show that variant's latest available checkpoint; $n{=}3$ seeds, except PDB L9-GearNet and AFDB L4-MPNN, $n{=}1$; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}}{44}{table.6.6}}
\newlabel{tab:proteina-13m}{{6.6}{44}{\textbf {On AFDB, REPA lifts the whole-set distribution cleanly; on PDB, the whole-set gains come with a more concentrated designable subset.} We compare rows within a data regime block, not across them. FPSD uses the training dataset as its reference in each case. Each cell is a mean over generation seeds; the per-seed min--max range is in Appendix~\ref {app:13m-ranges} (Table~\ref {tab:proteina-13m-ranges}). (Rows tagged with a step, e.g.\ \textit {\scriptsize (1.0M)}, show that variant's latest available checkpoint; $n{=}3$ seeds, except PDB L9-GearNet and AFDB L4-MPNN, $n{=}1$; $1{,}125$ backbones/seed, $250$ for designability.)}{table.6.6}{}}
